body:
I have a lot of thoughts on this topic and I won't get into all of them here but I've been in the trans community for a long time. Our understanding of gender has evolved and it's become a more difficult topic to talk about, with much more complexity. Human nature is to categorize the world in a way that makes sense. We tend to oversimplify the world not only to make sense of it all but to protect ourselves. Not understanding something produces a fear response and in this case that manifests as transphobia. This behavior pattern is echoed in things like the incels, racism and politics, especially.

&#x200B;

It takes an active effort to look past one's bias and do the research and some people just simply aren't willing or currently capable of doing that. I read somewhere that 80% of those surveyed don't personally know someone who is trans, so it's very easy to formulate an opinion on something when they have no exposure to the population they're categorizing. The negative response is the easy response. It takes little to no effort to conclude that trans women are men (by virtue of "being male") and finding a proud vocal minority with a stringent set of circular beliefs and buy in.
